From 25d7fd0da4462ca9d03ac20537b29feb5841862d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmage22 <danny.sui.lee@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:19:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] Windows: stop the compiler at inaccessible pages,
 restart it instead of dispatching its faults

D2R under mtrack freezes with every game thread idle and the GPU at 0%.
The freeze is a deadlock on CodeInvalidationMutex, reached like this:

1. CompileBlock holds the mutex shared and reads guest code through the
   decoder (and, with anon caching, the disk cache lookup's prefix decode
   and extents hash). The only guard on those reads is the executable
   interval list, which covers D2R's whole decrypted image as one range.
   Pages the protector has not decrypted yet are PAGE_NOACCESS inside it,
   so the read faults in FEX's own code.
2. ResetToConsistentState sees a fault that is neither JIT nor dispatcher
   code and passes it through to the guest. Before that it takes
   ThreadCreationMutex, which another thread may hold while it waits for
   the unique lock in HandleRWXAccessViolation (every SMC trap under
   mtrack). That is one deadlock. If it gets past that, the protector's
   handler re-enters the JIT on the same thread; WritePriorityMutex is
   non-recursive and parks a new reader whenever a writer is queued. That
   is the other. Either way nobody moves again.

SMC checks `none` sidesteps both because it never queues a writer, which is
why the hour-long run with it was clean, but it also stops FEX noticing
code changes.

Two changes:

- QueryExecutableRange asks the OS about the page and clamps the interval
  answer to the region that is actually readable right now (not committed,
  PAGE_NOACCESS or PAGE_GUARD ends the range). The decoder then stops a
  block in front of such a page and execution reaches it through a NoExec
  stub, which raises the fault the protector expects from JIT code, outside
  the compiler. The decoder re-queries once per block so a protection flip
  that never notified FEX is seen within one compile.
- If a guest read in the compiler still faults (a page flipped between the
  query and the read), the exception handler no longer dispatches it. It
  records the page, which the range query then reports as non-executable
  for the rest of that compile, and resumes at a restart point just after
  the lock is taken, using the same jump-buffer mechanism the JIT guard
  page already uses. Bounded to 64 restarts per block, after which the old
  behaviour applies. The check runs before ThreadCreationMutex is taken.

Both paths log, rate limited, as [xrange] and [compilefault] with
FEX_SILENTLOG=0. Built as 2610-d2rfix27.

